{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And it was on the next day.</b> According to the plain sense, on the next day after the feast-day, for on the day of the feast he was preoccupied with honoring his father-in-law and had no leisure to sit in judgment until the following day. Here Rashi explained: what is 'on the next day'? On the next day after his descent from the mountain. And if you ask: how could he sit to judge the people at that time, seeing that further on Rashi explained 'and Moses assembled' — on the next day after Yom HaKippurim, when Moses descended from the mountain — and they immediately began the work and finished on the 25th of Kislev of that year? One may answer: what it writes, <b>and Moses sat to judge the people</b>, does not refer to all Israel, but to those who were not occupied with the work. And so you shall explain at the end of parashat Tissa, where Rashi explained: after his descent from the mountain he would study Torah with those who were not occupied with the work. And what Rashi explained here, that this passage is not written in sequence for it was not stated until the second year — that is to reckon the years of the world, which begin from Tishrei, as he explained above in parashat Bo. And similarly the meaning of 'this passage is not written in sequence' — for it was not stated until the next day after Yom HaKippurim, which was in the second year, since it is stated here 'and Moses sent his father-in-law.' And we find in the journey of the standards — which was in the second year — that Moses said to him, 'We are journeying; do not leave us,' and in that episode he sent him, as it is written, 'And he said to him, I will not go.' And if this was before the giving of the Torah — meaning, if the passage of 'and it was on the next day' was stated before the giving of the Torah, and in the second year at the journey of the standards Moses again said to him, 'We are journeying' — the answer to this is: after he had sent him and he went, where do we find that Moses again said to him, 'We are journeying'? And what is written, 'the heart knows the bitterness of its soul, and into its joy no stranger shall mingle' — which our Sages expounded, that HaShem did not wish Yitro to share in the joy of Israel's reception of the Torah because he had not been with them in the affliction of bondage — even so, Moses did not send him away to his land at that time; rather, by divine word he withdrew and distanced himself from Israel until they received the Torah, and afterward he returned to Israel to study Torah, and he remained there until the second year at the journey of the standards, and then Moses said to him, 'We are journeying,' etc. And that is why it is written here 'and he went to his land' — implying that he was sent on another occasion and did not go to his land." "<b>And Moses sat to judge.</b> A term of tarrying, as in 'and you tarried in Kadesh.'" "<b>To judge the people.</b> Some interpret: of the mixed multitude, who were pressing claims against them for the spoils of their kinfolk. But this does not fit the sequence." "<b>From morning until evening.</b> One might think he judged the entire day — if so, when would the students sit to study Torah? Scripture teaches: <b>from the morning</b> — and not the entire morning, and not the entire day. And the Sages estimated: up to six hours — a baraita of Rabbi Eliezer."], :book :exodus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {0 "The comment weaves together multiple chronologies proposed by Rashi; the dense argumentation about whether Yitro left before or after Sinai is preserved as-is, including the prooftext from Proverbs 14:10."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:58:44.329485278Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}